 it's being dragged  it's being trolled  heavily online um was that the best  uh decision going into this holiday  weekend when people are struggling with  things  like putting gas in the car yeah it was  a curious decision to  tweet that tweet i mean people can  people know  by what they see at the gas tank by what  they see the grocery stores  people understand and fundamentally feel  what's what  inflation is is affecting by their own  reality by their own existence they  don't they're not going to be persuaded  by a white house  tweet touting a 16 savings i think byron  is right i think the federal government  the role of the federal government the  role of states in ending  these 300 per week additional  unemployment benefits you saw  more than two dozen states mostly run by  republicans  moved to end these additional  unemployment benefits in may or  announced that they were going to be  ending  in early june there's a lot more to  learn as we dig  into the numbers but i suspect that  we'll learn it's no coincidence that we  saw this strong jump in jobs at the same  time  that we've seen these two dozen states  cut those off or announced that they  were going to cut them off and it's  important to know  too that nearly 350 000 of those jobs  came in the hospitality industry which  had been really struggling to find  workers i think precisely because or at  least in one  respect because of these additional  unemployment benefits  so that's at least part of the equation  here  and again to put up the hard data so  people can see um great news that we're  talking about adding 850  000 jobs but it was unexpected that the  um unemployment rate went from 5.9  that's where it ended up the estimate  was going to be 5.6  charles your thoughts  i think that 5.9 is a little bit of a  puzzle i tend to see it as kind of  statistical noise because  the broader unemployment rate that  includes people who are discouraged from  seeking jobs or involuntarily on  part-time that number went down  so there was kind of a mixed picture  there  i think the big picture on the u.s  economy is  positive i think we went into an  incredibly deep hole in 2020 and  thanks to a huge amount of federal  stimulus and federal reserve  bank stimulus we're zooming back out of  it  and by all accounts that's going to  continue through 2021 and into the first  half  of 2022 most of the problems we have  actually seem to be the kinds of  problems you have  when you're growing so fast that you  can't keep  up factories can't find enough uh inputs  especially semiconductors that can't  find enough workers  their bottlenecks all over the place of  people trying to crank their restaurants  and hotels back up  and i'll end that showing in a little  bit of inflation i'll be very very  curious to see  whether this in 2022 is the issue people  vote on  or whether people regard this comeback  as something that was kind of inevitable  once the vaccinations occurred but you  know we can we can argue over the  the wobbles in the data but the big  picture is very positive  all right i want to get a very quick  comment from you guys in this lightning  round about the supreme court wrapping  up its terms so we get to your winners  and losers but this idea that it has  not performed as some of the best and  worst predictions were for the court it  hasn't exactly turned into the  handmaid's tale and by the way  i got a list today from the court of all  of the clerks that everybody has hired  for the fall so it doesn't look like  anyone's going anywhere  charles start with you and then we'll go  to steve then byron  well just quickly on the point you were  alluding to the non-retirement as  at least as of now of justice stephen  breyer  you know if he stays on the court  another term i think some of the people  who are practically demanding that he  quit  ought to do a little soul searching and  think to themselves was it such a smart  idea to try to humiliate  this very proud man in that way yeah it  didn't work with justice ginsburg a  quick comment from you  uh byron then steve or steven byron  yeah look i mean i think what we've  learned from from this particular term  is that  the the inclination to divide the court  into partisan terms which has dominated  the media over the past decade plus  uh really doesn't fit in many ways in  this particular court where you have  institutionalists conservatives liberals  what have you  byron well on this  this flower shop case that we're  discussing tonight i think it's kind of  consistent with one thing with the  supreme court is that  they don't want to solve the issue here  they  in in the cake shop case in colorado  they issued a very very narrow opinion  now they've completely ducked this one  at some point they're going to have to  come out with a ruling on this  but so far they're ducking it yeah i got  to do it on the merits okay very quick  hey sean hannity here hey  click here to subscribe to fox news  youtube page  and catch our hottest interviews and  most compelling analysis  you will not get it anywhere else

